WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:16.820
Black holes are the monsters of the universe,
terrifying cosmic beasts that devour all they

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:25.310
encounter. But black holes scare scientists
for very different reasons.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.230
They challenge our theories to the breaking
point.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.230
This is at the forefront of theoretical
physics.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.440
When it comes to the detailed nature of black
holes.

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.950
It would not surprise me if we got it all
wrong.

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:52.630
The science of black holes is so challenging
that some scientists question whether they

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:53.680
exist at all.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.330
Despite their fearsome reputation.

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.630
We've never actually seen one.

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:08.750
Black holes are everywhere.

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:09.930
They're all over the universe.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.340
They're all throughout our galaxy.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.930
But that doesn't mean that they're easy to
find.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.740
They're black and space is black.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.120
And black on black is kind of hard to see in
a picture of space.

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:27.860
This is paradoxical because scientists
believe black holes are born and some of the

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:30.170
brightest explosions in the universe.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:38.720
Rising from the corpses of detonated stars
many times larger than our sun.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:47.270
The star that burns for 10 million years
collapses to form a black hole in a period of

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:48.290
seconds.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:57.260
As it collapses, the outer region of the star
hits the core, triggering a huge explosion, a

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:06.610
supernova. We see the bang, but not what's
left

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:15.710
behind. A dead core with the enormous mass
of the star crushed down into an

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:18.170
infinitesimal, tiny area.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.720
From this minuscule high mass core.

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:31.150
A black hole is born.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:37.270
The flow of gravity is so strong that nothing
can escape.

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:38.530
Not even light.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:47.180
But how can scientists claim that black holes
exist if we can't even see them?

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.890
You could say that about the existence of the
atom.

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.880
We knew they had existed for decades,
centuries before.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.110
We had actually seen one in some sort of
imaging device.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.290
And so it's the same sort of thing with
black holes.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:10.180
Just because you can't see it doesn't mean
it's not there.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.610
Not seeing black holes, but knowing they're
there is a possibility, just like we know

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:17.710
that wind is there even though we can't see
air.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:26.230
Air is invisible, but when the wind blows,
its effects can be measured.

00:03:27.880 --> 00:03:29.680
It's the same with black holes.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:32.290
You just need to know what to look for.

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.100
Black holes are gravitational giants of the
universe.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:57.100
But we found only circumstantial evidence
that they exist to make it a slam dunk.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:03.130
Cosmologists are listening for proof in the
hidden world of gravitational waves.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:08.800
There are gravitational waves going through
this room all the time.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.000
Every time I move my hands like I just did.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:12.820
I create gravitational waves.

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:18.670
The problem is gravity is so weak that you
don't detect those gravitational waves.

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:26.240
In order to detect those disturbances in
space and time, you have to have cataclysmic

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:28.730
events involving massive objects.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:35.780
Black holes are some of the densest objects
in the universe.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:41.750
So we should be able to hear and measure the
waves created when they collide.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:49.040
Legault The long interferometry
gravitational wave observatory, listens for

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:52.790
waves that can come from over a billion
light years away.

00:04:56.390 --> 00:05:01.130
In 2017, Legault heard an enormous crash.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:07.140
Two very massive objects collided at near the
speed of light.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:11.160
And one of the most energetic events that we
have ever witnessed in the history of

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:11.970
humankind.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:21.310
Two ultra heavy, ultra dense objects whirl
around each other, hurling powerful

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:23.650
gravitational waves through space.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:30.390
The closer they fall toward each other, the
more gravitational energy they throw out.

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:37.180
Finally they collide in one of the most
violent events in the universe.

00:05:40.350 --> 00:05:46.500
The smash sends out immense gravitational
waves that ripple across intergalactic space

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:51.270
until eventually Legault detects them.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:59.100
Listening to a gravitational wave is like
listening to a musical instrument.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:05.640
If it's making certain tones or certain
vibrations, you can figure out the size of

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:08.010
the musical instrument, the type of the
musical instrument.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.020
Who's playing the musical instrument?

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:16.240
The thing that's really amazing about the
Lego detection is it allowed us to measure

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:19.390
the mass of these objects and how quickly
they coalesce together.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:22.960
So we actually have an idea how dense they
must have been.

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.900
And with modern physics, we say, well, it
has to be a black hole.

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:28.750
But the question is, have we missed
something?

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:36.070
The information gathered by Legault is
groundbreaking.

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:43.600
But some scientists think that the
gravitational waves could have come not from

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.720
black holes, but from something even more
mysterious.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:56.730
It's possible that what we identify as black
holes in our universe are really another

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:58.590
object like Gravity Star.

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:01.950
Possible. There's a capital P on that
possibility.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:15.090
I grab a star is what scientists call an
exotic, compact object.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:22.410
This bizarre theoretical body has exactly
the same mass and gravitational pull as a

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:27.030
black hole. But it's made of exotic matter.

00:07:27.570 --> 00:07:33.030
A gravel star would be impossible to see
with the naked eye, but because it forms

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:38.670
differently than a black hole, it has a
strange, incredibly dense surface.

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:47.410
In the formation of what we think of as the
black hole, the catastrophic gravitational

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:49.080
collapse of a dense object.

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:53.290
Maybe it doesn't go all the way down to
become an infinitely dense point.

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:58.840
Instead, maybe there's some interaction that
prevents the formation of the black hole, and

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:03.970
instead you have a tight little dense ball,
which is what we call a gravity star.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:11.640
So the Lego data could be the signature of
two black holes colliding, but it also could

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.100
be the signature of two gravest stars
colliding.

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:17.730
Right now, we can't tell them apart.

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:33.970
So for now, gravitational waves have led to a
dead end in the hunt for black holes.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:40.950
We can't be completely sure that we're
hearing them and we already know we can't see

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:45.520
them. But what about the mayhem they leave
behind?

00:08:49.410 --> 00:08:54.450
Even though you can't see the black hole
itself, it's going to leave behind a trail of

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:57.120
destruction. And that is something you can
see.

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:09.280
You. By picking apart a cosmic crime scene,
can scientists finally solve the mystery of

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:10.450
black holes?

